<Base title="Who I am — Tawanda Moyo" includePersonSchema>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top: 3.5rem;">
    <p>
      I grew up and work in Zimbabwe. I studied Electronic Engineering at the
      Harare Institute of Technology, and that training is load-bearing: I
      design systems from hardware realities upward, then build the software
      layers that make them reliable in production. My trade is web
      development and technical writing — client software, documentation
      systems, API references — with editorial contributions to the
      <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/">WHATWG HTML Standard</a>, the
      <a href="https://matrix.org">Matrix protocol website</a>, and
      <a href="https://inspect.aisi.org.uk/">Inspect</a>, the UK AI Security
      Institute’s evaluations framework, along the way.
      My favourite way to learn a system is to land a change in a large
      codebase I didn't write: the most recent was
      <a href="/implementing-javascript-proposal-spidermonkey/">implementing a
      TC39 proposal in SpiderMonkey</a>, Firefox's JavaScript engine.
      Explaining a system and building it have never felt like separate jobs
      to me: the explanation is usually where I find out whether the system is
      any good.
    </p>
    <p>
      These days I am moving that craft toward AI safety — compute governance,
      evaluations, forecasting, and the slower question of how people keep
      their agency as machines get capable. I completed the ML4Good technical
      track in 2026, served as a Tech Fellow at the Living Together Institute,
      and the <a href="https://africacomputetracker.org">Africa Compute
      Tracker</a> is the current expression of the work. I care about this
      partly because I write from a place that has already lived through
      several kinds of quiet disempowerment, and noticing early is a skill my
      country teaches.
    </p>
    <p>
      Away from the screen: essays, history, the Zhuangzi, a morning jog, long
      quiet mornings. Montaigne asked <em>que sais-je?</em> — what do I
      know? — and I try to build and write in that spirit.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are here deciding whether to work with me, the test I would apply
      to myself has two halves: could I land a substantial change in a serious
      codebase I didn't write, and do I have considered thoughts on AI and the
      future? For the first, start with the
      <a href="/implementing-javascript-proposal-spidermonkey/">SpiderMonkey
      work</a> and <a href="/#work">the rest of the work</a> — all of it real
      and inspectable. For the second, read the
      <a href="/#essays">essays</a>. And
      <a href="mailto:moyotawanda@gmail.com">email</a> reaches me quickly; I
      read everything.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
